WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.020
- You can tell people
who've grown up with this

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:05.010
when they say plantain as
opposed to the incorrect

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.050
pronunciation plantain.
- Plantain.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:08.172
- I don't know what that is.
- To be clear.

00:00:08.172 --> 00:00:09.607
- We don't know what that is.
(laughs)

00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:12.640
- Hi, I'm Nicholette and I'm
representing Trinidad. Bop bop!

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.235
- Hi, I'm Nneka and I'm
representing Jamaica.

00:00:15.235 --> 00:00:18.120
- I'm Anthony and I'm from
South Trinedad and Tebago.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.120
- I'm Kat. I'm from Kingston, Jamaica.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.570
- So, I was born in Trinidad.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.230
I stayed there for two and a half years,

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.830
and I moved to Brooklyn,
and that's where I grew up.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.200
- I was born in Florida, United States,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.910
and I moved to Jamaica when I was young.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:32.610
And I've been back and forth ever since.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.720
- I actually moved from Trinidad in 2004.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.300
- I grew up all my life in Kingston

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.930
and just traveled a bunch 'cause I have

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.280
a lot of family, like in
Florida, most Jamaicans do.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.750
- As far as Jamaican snacks,
they are very similar.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.330
- They're pretty
interchangeable, you know you

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:50.110
have them growing up when
you're in high school.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:51.680
- There are some that
I haven't had though,

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.670
I'm excited to see what this is about.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.530
- Yeah, I'm excited to
try some of these snacks.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:00.290
Drink sorrel, sorrel is like
Christmas, ginger beer is also

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:02.520
a similar beverage, ya know.
- Yeah, very similar.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.010
- So I just got this National Spice Bun.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.040
This is very exciting,
because it's kind of a staple

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.680
in Jamaican households.
- Okay.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.350
- The flavors are kinda
very homey, very familiar,

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.330
kinda the flavors that
your mom cooks with.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:17.810
The smells that you smell at home,

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.410
it's kind of like a warm hug of a snack.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:22.560
- I'm excited about that,
I've never had bun before.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.890
- Smells like my
childhood, like definitely.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:25.880
- It's like a spicy bun.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.050
- Yeah, like nutmeg, allspice.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:28.883
- [Anthony] Yeah, yeah.

00:01:28.883 --> 00:01:31.580
- It's kind of like a
dessert bun. You know?

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.610
But, people eat it like
a meal, like I remember

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:35.940
growing up if you never had a lot

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:36.849
of lunch money,
- Yeah.

00:01:36.849 --> 00:01:38.970
- you get a bun and cheese
and it's like very filling.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.020
- So we have some fried plantains here.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.301
This is classic I'm
sure you know if you're

00:01:43.301 --> 00:01:44.134
- Yeah.
- from a Caribbean household.

00:01:44.134 --> 00:01:46.030
I mean, literally it's mandatory.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.530
- Exactly.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.480
- This to me, reminds me
of Sunday lunch, you know?

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.950
Water crystal, callaloo,
rice, stewed chicken,

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.410
every Sunday, you know?

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:56.880
- This one is lookin' all right.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.046
- Oh yeah let's go.

00:01:58.046 --> 00:01:59.225
You do it.
- Yeah, okay okay.

00:01:59.225 --> 00:02:02.742
- Enjoy. We're just saying
Jamaica, full of joy.

00:02:02.742 --> 00:02:05.810
(laughing)

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.218
- Mmmm.

00:02:07.218 --> 00:02:09.980
- It's good. It's a good reaction.

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:12.030
- Wow, I didn't know what
to expect, but this is good.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.620
- Right? The texture.
- Mm-hmm.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.540
- The bun is good, it really
reminds me of a sweet bun

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:17.849
that we used to make in Trinidad.

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:19.520
- So in Trinidad, we have
something called sweet bread.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.590
It's kinda similar. They
use, like raisins and fruits,

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.960
but it has a similar
texture, similar taste to it.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:26.906
- Oh cool.
- Yeah.

00:02:26.906 --> 00:02:29.350
I don't know we might have to kill this.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.880
(laughing)

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.872
- This is good plantain.
- [Nicholette] Yes, it's good.

00:02:32.872 --> 00:02:33.705
- It's good.
- What do you normally

00:02:33.705 --> 00:02:34.538
eat plantain with?

00:02:34.538 --> 00:02:36.544
Like, type of dish.
- Anything!

00:02:36.544 --> 00:02:37.783
(laughs)
- True, true.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.093
- By itself, for breakfast,
for lunch, for dinner.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.180
It's like around the clock, you can count

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.500
on plantain to be there for you.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.333
- Plantain bae.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:55.320
- So, like, this wouldn't
be my choice of plantain.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:56.153
- Why not?

00:02:56.153 --> 00:02:57.520
- Because it is not ripe enough for me.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.950
I would leave it alone
like for extra week,

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:02.260
- Yeah.
- and make the skin get

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:03.780
blacker and then I'd fry it.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.550
- Okay so, I have this weird thing

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.140
where I tend to not
combine sweet with savory.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.330
So, while I know that
everyone eats plantain

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.630
all the time, but I do not.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.320
But I also respect and
appreciate the tradition.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.153
- Yeah.

00:03:20.153 --> 00:03:22.020
- And that's why I'm eating it right now.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.250
- Interesting.
- Because I'm not

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.425
gonna come on here as a
Jamaican and not eat plantain.

00:03:26.425 --> 00:03:28.603
(laughter)
- I respect it.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.130
- In front of me I have some
tamarind ball, I'm so excited

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:35.480
I love anything Tamarind,
I like bittersweet flavors.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.060
This is a classic, I'm very excited,

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.380
and I'm excited for you to try.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.280
- What's interesting about this package

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.017
of Tamarind Balls, is if
you look closely it says,

00:03:45.017 --> 00:03:48.123
"Product of Jamaica".
- It says Jamaica, it does.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.460
- Anyway.
(laughs)

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:55.120
- I have the coconut drops.
I don't eat coconut drops.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.050
I drink coconut water, but
I don't really consume,

00:03:58.050 --> 00:03:59.720
I think it's like too chewy for me.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:00.553
- I gotcha.

00:04:00.553 --> 00:04:02.222
- So it's like a texture thing.

00:04:02.222 --> 00:04:03.570
- And what's interesting about these is

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:07.500
that nothing about this looks like a drop.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.410
- So that's interesting.
- It doesn't.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:12.740
- They're always kind of
homemade looking, and I

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:14.360
